    "order": 2,
    "practice": "Abstracting and Generalizing",
    "why": "After interpreting the tape diagram, the student must recognize the underlying structure—5 equal groups with an additional 4 added to each group, totaling 99—and generalize this pattern to identify which real-world situations match this structure. This involves identifying commonality across different contexts (e.g., bouquets, babysitting, marbles) and extending the reasoning to determine which scenarios fit the same mathematical model.",
    "likely_errors": "A student might fail to see the structural similarity across different contexts, especially if the scenario is less familiar (e.g., marbles or muffins), or incorrectly assume that any situation with 5, 4, and 99 must match. This is moderately likely for students who lack experience in identifying abstract structures across contexts."
  },
  {
    "order": 3,
    "practice": "Justifying and Proving",
    "why": "The student must evaluate each answer choice and justify why it does or does not match the tape diagram's structure by checking whether the total can be expressed as (number of groups × amount per group) + (number of groups × extra per group). This requires constructing a logical argument based on the model and verifying the mathematical consistency of each scenario.",
